  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nModels through the weekend are is good agreement with the upper \nhigh shifting westward and ending up in the KS/OK border region \nwith a ridge axis cutting through the CWA. Currently we have some \nof the highest PW's of the monsoon season in place with 1.4\" on \n00Z EPZ sounding. With pretty weak flow over the area, this \nmoisture remains in place through Sat. The issue will be a little \nsubsidence inversion showing up on forecast soundings around \n400-500mb and that ridge axis. \n\nThere will be an MCV developing overnight and bringing some \nshowers and a few thunderstorms to the CWA. Looks like most precip\nshould be over by sunrise Fri, but there will be residual cloud \ncover at least through the morning which will likely limit heating\nsomewhat into the afternoon. As happened today, these morning \nclouds could limit afternoon storms as well. GFS and CAMs are \nkeeping coverage pretty limited and over the northern and eastern \nareas. EC is a little more aggressive with storms continuing into \nthe RGV and far west TX after 00Z. There is agreement there will \nbe some LL convergence over Hudspeth around 00Z which could send \noutflows further west and trigger additional storms. Previous \nforecast seems to cover this pretty well and will not stray too \nfar from that. High temperatures will continue to be warm with mid\n80s to mid 90s for the lowlands depending on cloud cover.\n\nA similar pattern for Sat, except there is a better low level \nwind flow for storms to develop over the Sacs and then outflows \npushing storms onto the lowlands. The ridge axis and subsidence \ninversion will limit storm coverage but at least isolated(20%) \ncoverage looks warranted for eastern areas. temperatures look \nsimilar to Fri. \n\nFor Sun/Mon, the ridge axis pushes a little further west and \nstarts to bring in some drier air to eastern areas. Expect most \nstorm coverage here to be focused west of the Continental Divide. \nThese also look like the warmest days with some areas approaching \n100 in the lowest elevations on Sunday. \n\nTue-Thu of next week we are seeing some differences in the \nmodels. The EC keeps upper high the dominant weather feature over \nthe area while the GFS has the high further east and allows a \nweakness in the upper flow to move in from Mexico. Ensemble means \nof both are more similar and the upper weakness shown on the \noperational GFS is not as noticeable in it's mean. There will be a\nnice ESE flow pattern though, which will bring a return to some \nbetter moisture, so will keep NBM low end PoPs going with temps \nremaining on a very slow downward trend.",
